::ALG2_U01_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x) = 2x + 7, what is f(4)? {
~11 #This adds 4 and 7 but does not multiply by 2.
=15 #Correct. Substitute 4 for x: 2(4) + 7 = 15.
~16 #This multiplies 4 by 4 instead of using 2x + 7.
~18 #This adds incorrectly after substituting.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If g(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 5, what is g(6)? {
~25 #This squares 5 instead of evaluating 6 squared minus 5.
~30 #This is close, but 36 - 5 equals 31.
~41 #This adds 5 instead of subtracting 5.
=31 #Correct. g(6) = 6<sup>2</sup> - 5 = 36 - 5 = 31.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If h(x) = 10 - 3x, what is h(2)? {
=4 #Correct. h(2) = 10 - 3(2) = 10 - 6 = 4.
~16 #This adds 3(2) instead of subtracting it.
~7 #This subtracts 3 once instead of subtracting 3 times 2.
~14 #This uses 10 + 2 + 2 instead of the function rule.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If p(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 2x, what is p(3)? {
~9 #This only finds 3 squared and omits 2x.
~11 #This uses 3 squared plus 2 instead of plus 2(3).
=15 #Correct. p(3) = 3<sup>2</sup> + 2(3) = 9 + 6 = 15.
~18 #This adds 9 and 9, which does not match the rule.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does f(8) = 12 mean? {
=When the input is 8, the output is 12. #Correct. f(8) names the output for input 8.
~The input 12 gives output 8. #This reverses the input and output.
~The function is multiplied by 8 to get 12. #Function notation does not mean multiplication.
~The domain is only 12. #This statement gives one input-output pair, not the full domain.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which relation is a function? {
~(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4) #Input 1 has two different outputs.
~(0, 5), (0, 7), (1, 9) #Input 0 has two different outputs.
~(-2, 1), (3, 4), (3, 8) #Input 3 has two different outputs.
=(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6) #Correct. Each input has exactly one output.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which relation is not a function? {
~(-1, 4), (0, 4), (1, 4) #Repeated outputs are allowed, and each input appears once.
=(5, 1), (5, 2), (6, 3) #Correct. Input 5 has two different outputs.
~(2, 5), (3, 6), (4, 7) #Each input has exactly one output.
~(7, 0), (8, 0), (9, 1) #Each input has exactly one output.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>For (-3, 8), (0, 2), (5, 2), what is the domain? {
~8, 2 #This lists output values, so it describes the range.
~-3, 8, 0 #This mixes input and output values.
=-3, 0, 5 #Correct. The domain is the set of x-values.
~0, 2, 5 #This includes an output value and omits -3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>For (-4, 6), (1, 6), (3, 9), what is the range? {
~-4, 1, 3 #This is the domain, not the range.
~6, 6, 9 #The repeated output should be listed once in the range.
~-4, 6, 9 #This mixes an input with output values.
=6, 9 #Correct. The range lists output values once.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A table has inputs -1, 0, 2 and outputs 4, 1, 7. What is f(2)? {
=7 #Correct. The output paired with input 2 is 7.
~2 #This gives the input rather than the output.
~4 #This is the output for input -1, not input 2.
~1 #This is the output for input 0, not input 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A table has inputs -2, 1, 4 and outputs 9, 0, 9. Which statement is true? {
~The relation is not a function because 9 repeats. #Repeated outputs are allowed in a function.
~The domain is 9 and 0 only. #That set describes the output values, not the domain.
=The relation is a function because each input has one output. #Correct. Each input is paired with exactly one output.
~The range is -2, 1, and 4. #That set describes the input values, not the range.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A graph crosses the y-axis at 5. Which point must be on the graph? {
~(5, 0) #This would be an x-intercept at 5.
=(0, 5) #Correct. A y-intercept occurs where x = 0.
~(5, 5) #This point is not necessarily on the y-axis.
~(0, -5) #This would mean the y-intercept is -5.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A graph has x-intercepts at -4 and 1. What are the zeros? {
=-4 and 1 #Correct. Zeros are the x-values where the graph meets the x-axis.
~4 and -1 #These are sign reversals of the x-intercepts.
~0 and 1 #Only one listed value is an x-intercept.
~-4 and 0 #Only one listed value is an x-intercept.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement describes an increasing function on an interval? {
~As x-values move left to right, y-values fall. #This describes decreasing behavior.
~The graph crosses the y-axis. #A y-intercept does not describe increasing behavior.
=As x-values move left to right, y-values rise. #Correct. Increasing means outputs rise as inputs increase.
~The graph has no x-intercepts. #Zeros do not determine whether a function is increasing.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement describes a decreasing function on an interval? {
~Outputs rise as inputs increase. #This describes increasing behavior.
~Outputs stay the same for every input. #This describes a constant interval.
~Inputs repeat with different outputs. #That describes a relation that is not a function.
=Outputs fall as inputs increase. #Correct. Decreasing means y-values fall as x-values move left to right.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A function has a maximum value of 12. What does that mean? {
~The only input is 12. #A maximum is an output value, not necessarily an input.
=The greatest output value is 12. #Correct. A maximum value is the largest y-value.
~The function is always 12. #A maximum does not mean every output is 12.
~The graph crosses the x-axis at 12. #That would describe a zero at x = 12, not a maximum value.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A function has a minimum value of -3. What does that mean? {
~The least input value is -3. #A minimum value describes output, not input.
~The x-intercept is -3. #An x-intercept is where the output is 0.
=The least output value is -3. #Correct. A minimum value is the smallest y-value.
~The domain is -3 only. #A minimum value does not give the full domain.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If q(x) = -2x + 9, what is q(-1)? {
=11 #Correct. q(-1) = -2(-1) + 9 = 2 + 9 = 11.
~7 #This substitutes 1 instead of -1.
~-11 #This has sign errors when multiplying -2 and -1.
~8 #This adds -1 directly instead of substituting into -2x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>If r(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 1, what is r(-3)? {
~-8 #This treats (-3)<sup>2</sup> as -9 and then adds 1.
=10 #Correct. r(-3) = (-3)<sup>2</sup> + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10.
~7 #This uses 3<sup>2</sup> - 2 instead of the given rule.
~-10 #This keeps an incorrect negative after squaring.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>For k(x) = 4x - 12, which input makes k(x) = 0? {
~0 #k(0) = -12, not 0.
~2 #k(2) = -4, not 0.
~12 #k(12) = 36, not 0.
=3 #Correct. 4x - 12 = 0 gives x = 3.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>For m(x) = x + 6, which input makes m(x) = 0? {
~0 #m(0) = 6, not 0.
=-6 #Correct. -6 + 6 = 0.
~6 #m(6) = 12, not 0.
~-1 #m(-1) = 5, not 0.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which set could represent the domain of a function shown by ordered pairs? {
=The x-values only #Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
~The y-values only #The domain is not the output values.
~All values in the ordered pairs #Domain includes inputs only, not both inputs and outputs.
~Only values where y = 0 #Those are zeros, not the full domain.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which set could represent the range of a function shown by ordered pairs? {
~The x-values only #The x-values form the domain.
~Only repeated inputs #Repeated inputs are not the range.
~Only values where x = 0 #That describes a y-intercept condition, not the range.
=The y-values only #Correct. The range is the set of output values.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student says the relation (2, 3), (2, 3), (4, 5) is not a function because 2 repeats. What is the best correction? {
~It is not a function because repeated inputs are never allowed. #Repeated identical ordered pairs do not assign different outputs.
~It is a function because every output repeats. #The outputs do not all repeat, and repeated outputs are not the reason.
=It can still be a function because input 2 does not have different outputs. #Correct. The repeated input is paired with the same output.
~It is not a function because the range contains 3. #The value 3 in the range does not prevent a relation from being a function.
}

::ALG2_U01_L01_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student says f(3) = 10 means the point (10, 3) is on the graph. What is the best correction? {
=The point should be (3, 10) because 3 is the input and 10 is the output. #Correct. f(3) = 10 corresponds to the ordered pair (3, 10).
~The student is correct because output comes first. #In function notation, the input is first in the ordered pair.
~The point should be (0, 10) because 10 is a y-intercept. #The statement does not say the input is 0.
~The point should be (3, 0) because 3 is a zero. #The output is 10, not 0.
}
